Below is a **complete, step‑by‑step recipe** for loading a **`.pmtiles`** archive (vector or raster) into a MapLibre GL JS map.  
It works whether you are using a bundler (npm/webpack/parcel) or just plain `<script>` tags on a static page.

---

## 1️⃣ What you need to know

| Item | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| **PMTiles** – a single‑file container for tiled raster or vector data (MVT) | MapLibre can’t read the file format directly, but the **`pmtiles` JavaScript library** implements a custom URL protocol (`pmtiles://`) that streams the needed tiles from the archive. |
| **MapLibre GL JS ≥ 2.4** | The library already knows how to treat a `pmtiles://` URL as a normal tile source, so you only have to register the protocol once. |
| **CORS** | The `.pmtiles` file must be served with `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin:*` (or your domain) if it lives on a different origin than the page. |
| **File type** | • **Vector** → `type: "vector"` (MVT) <br>• **Raster** → `type: "raster"` (PNG/JPEG/WebP) |

---

## 2️⃣ Install / load the `pmtiles` library

### a) Using npm / a bundler (recommended)

```bash
npm install pmtiles maplibre-gl
```

```js
// index.js (or any module)
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import { PMTiles } from 'pmtiles';
```

### b) Using CDN script tags (quick demo)

```html
<!-- MapLibre GL JS -->
<link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@2.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

<!-- PMTiles library (tiny, ~10 KB gzipped) -->
<script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/pmtiles@2.7.0/dist/pmtiles.min.js"></script>
```

> **Tip:** The CDN version exports a global `PMTiles` constructor, so you can skip the `import` line.

---

## 3️⃣ Register the `pmtiles://` protocol once (before you create the map)

```js
// ---------------------------------------------------
// 1️⃣ Create a PMTiles object that points at your file
// ---------------------------------------------------
const pmtilesUrl = 'https://example.com/tiles/my‑data.pmtiles'; // <-- change this

// If you are using the CDN version, `PMTiles` is already on the window.
const pmtiles = new PMTiles(pmtilesUrl);

// ---------------------------------------------------
// 2️⃣ Tell MapLibre how to resolve "pmtiles://" URLs
// ---------------------------------------------------
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', (request) => {
  // `request` is an object like { url: "pmtiles://my-data.pmtiles/12/345/678.mvt" }
  // The PMTiles instance knows how to fetch the right byte range.
  return pmtiles.get(request);
});
```

> **What’s happening?**  
> `maplibregl.addProtocol()` registers a **custom fetch handler**. Whenever MapLibre sees a URL that starts with `pmtiles://`, it calls the handler, which in turn asks the `PMTiles` instance to return a `Response` (or a `Promise` that resolves to one). The library reads the archive’s internal index, performs a **byte‑range HTTP request** to the original `.pmtiles` file, and streams back just the tile you asked for.

---

## 4️⃣ Create the MapLibre map

```js
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div> in your HTML
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style you like
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

---

## 5️⃣ Add the `.pmtiles` source

### 5‑a) Vector tiles (MVT)

```js
map.addSource('my-pmtiles-vector', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The "pmtiles://" URL must contain the *archive name* (the part after the protocol)
  // followed by the tile path pattern that the archive expects.
  // Most PMTiles archives are built with the default pattern:
  //   {z}/{x}/{y}.pbf   (or .mvt)
  // So we just give the archive name – MapLibre will append the tile coordinates.
  url: 'pmtiles://my-data.pmtiles'   // <-- the part after "pmtiles://" is the *filename* you passed to PMTiles()
});
```

> **Why no `{z}/{x}/{y}` in the URL?**  
> The `pmtiles` protocol handler automatically expands the request to something like  
> `pmtiles://my-data.pmtiles/12/345/678.mvt`. The archive’s internal index tells it where that tile lives inside the file.

#### Add a layer that uses the source

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-pmtiles-layer',
  type: 'fill',                     // or 'line', 'symbol', etc.
  source: 'my-pmtiles-vector',
  'source-layer': 'my_layer_name', // <-- the name of the layer inside the .pmtiles (check with `pmtiles info` or your source data)
  paint: {
    'fill-color': '#ff6600',
    'fill-opacity': 0.5
  }
});
```

### 5‑b) Raster tiles (PNG/JPEG/WebP)

If the archive contains raster tiles, just change the source type:

```js
map.addSource('my-pmtiles-raster', {
  type: 'raster',
  url: 'pmtiles://my-raster.pmtiles',
  tileSize: 256,          // 256 or 512 – whatever you built the archive with
  rasterOpacity: 1.0
});

map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-pmtiles-raster-layer',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'my-pmtiles-raster',
  paint: {}
});
```

