## 5. Advanced patterns

### 5.1 In‑memory tile cache

If you already have the tile data in a `Map<string,ArrayBuffer>` (e.g., from IndexedDB), you can return it synchronously:

```js
const tileCache = new Map(); // key = `${z}/${x}/${y}`

maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', (params) => {
  const key = `${params.zoom}/${params.x}/${params.y}`;
  const cached = tileCache.get(key);
  if (cached) return cached;               // sync return → no Promise needed
  // otherwise fall back to network
  return fetch(`https://…/${key}.pbf`).then(r => r.arrayBuffer());
});
```

### 5.2 Using a custom XHR library (e.g., Axios)

```js
import axios from 'axios';

maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', (params, abort) => {
  const sourceUrl = `https://my.server/${params.zoom}/${params.x}/${params.y}.pbf`;

  // Axios supports AbortSignal from v1.2+:
  return axios.get(sourceUrl, {
    responseType: 'arraybuffer',
    signal: abort.signal,
  }).then(res => res.data);
});
```

### 5.3 Streaming large raster tiles (WebP, JPEG)

If you want to stream the response directly (useful for very large raster tiles), just return the `Response` object:

```js
maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', (params, abort) => {
  const url = `https://my.raster.server/${params.zoom}/${params.x}/${params.y}.webp`;
  return fetch(url, {signal: abort.signal}); // ← return the Response
});
```

The map will read the body as a blob internally.

