## TL;DR  
**PMTiles is great for static, pre‑generated raster or vector tiles (e.g., a basemap).**  
For a layer that changes every 30 seconds you’ll want a *dynamic* tile source (or a real‑time GeoJSON/WebSocket feed) on top of the static basemap.  

Below is a quick decision matrix, a recommended architecture, and concrete implementation tips so you can get a fast, scalable bus‑tracker map up and running.

---

## 1. What PMTiles Actually Is

| Feature | PMTiles (and its sibling MBTiles) |
|---------|-----------------------------------|
| **File format** | Single‑file container (SQLite‑like) that stores raster or vector tiles. |
| **Read‑only** | Tiles are written once (or rarely) and then served as static assets. |
| **Caching** | Extremely efficient – the whole archive can be cached by CDNs, browsers, or even served from a CDN edge. |
| **Update model** | To change a tile you must rewrite the file (or create a new version). |
| **Typical use‑case** | Basemaps, historic data, offline apps, static thematic layers. |

**Bottom line:** PMTiles shines when the tile set is *static* or changes only on a schedule of hours‑to‑days. It is **not** designed for “live” data that updates every few seconds.

---

## 2. Why 30‑second Bus Positions Don’t Fit PMTiles

| Requirement | How PMTiles behaves |
|-------------|---------------------|
| **Update frequency** | You’d need to rebuild the whole tile set (or at least the tiles that contain the moving buses) every 30 s → massive I/O, CPU, and storage churn. |
| **Latency** | Regenerating tiles adds processing time; the user may see stale positions. |
| **Scalability** | Re‑packing a multi‑GB PMTiles file every half‑minute will quickly saturate your server and break CDN caching. |
| **Granularity** | Buses are point features; you’d be generating many tiny vector tiles just to move a few points. |

Result: **PMTiles is a poor fit for the live bus layer**.

---

## 3. Recommended Architecture

```
+-------------------+          +-------------------+          +-------------------+
|  Browser / Client | <--WS--> |  Real‑time API    | <--SQL--> |  PostGIS (bus)    |
+-------------------+          +-------------------+          +-------------------+
        |                                 |
        |  (static basemap tiles)         |
        v                                 v
+-------------------+          +-------------------+
|  PMTiles / CDN    | <--HTTP-->|  Tile Server (e.g.,|
|  (basemap)        |          |  pg_tileserv,     |
+-------------------+          |  TileServer GL)   |
                               +-------------------+
```

### 3.1. Split the problem into two layers

| Layer | How to serve it | Why |
|------|----------------|-----|
| **Basemap** (streets, terrain, parcels, etc.) | **PMTiles** (or any static tile source) served from a CDN. | One‑time generation → ultra‑fast, cheap, offline‑friendly. |
| **Live bus positions** | **Dynamic vector tiles** *or* **GeoJSON over WebSocket/REST**. | Updated every 30 s (or faster) without rebuilding the whole tile archive. |

You can still use **vector tiles** for the bus layer if you want the same styling pipeline as the basemap, but you generate them on‑the‑fly rather than pre‑packing them.

---

## 4. Options for the Live Bus Layer

| Option | Stack | Pros | Cons | When to pick it |
|--------|-------|------|------|-----------------|
| **Dynamic vector tiles (on‑the‑fly)** | `pg_tileserv` (Go) / `TileServer GL` (Node) / `t-rex` (Rust) | • Directly reads from PostGIS <br>• Caches per‑tile (e.g., Redis) <br>• Works with MapLibre GL/Mapbox GL | • Tile generation cost per request (mitigated by caching) | • You want map‑style consistency, clustering, or client‑side filtering. |
| **GeoJSON endpoint (REST)** | Express/FastAPI/Django → `SELECT ... FROM buses` | • Simpler to implement <br>• Very low latency for < 10 k points <br>• Easy to push via WebSocket | • No built‑in tiling → client must handle clustering/decluttering | • Small fleet (< 5 k) or you need real‑time push (WebSocket). |
| **WebSocket / Server‑Sent Events** | `socket.io`, `ws`, `FastAPI` + `uvicorn` | • True push model (no polling) <br>• Minimal bandwidth (only deltas) | • Requires a persistent connection, more dev overhead | • You need sub‑second updates or want to animate smoothly. |
| **Hybrid** (static basemap + WebSocket bus points) | Combine any static tile source + a lightweight JSON push | • Best of both worlds: fast basemap, real‑time overlay | • Two separate data flows to manage | • Most production‑grade trackers. |

### 4.1. Example: `pg_tileserv` + MapLibre GL

1. **Install pg_tileserv** (single binary, no extra config):
   ```bash
   # on the DB server
   wget https://github.com/CrunchyData/pg_tileserv/releases/download/v1.0.0/pg_tileserv_linux_amd64.tar.gz
   tar xzf pg_tileserv_linux_amd64.tar.gz
   ./pg_tileserv --port 7800 --db "host=localhost dbname=transport user=tileserv"
   ```

2. **Expose a view** that returns the latest bus positions as a *vector tile*:
   ```sql
   CREATE OR REPLACE VIEW v_bus_positions AS
   SELECT
       bus_id,
       ST_Transform(geom, 3857) AS geom,
       speed,
       heading,
       last_update
   FROM bus_positions
   WHERE last_update > now() - interval '2 minutes';
   ```

   `pg_tileserv` automatically serves `/tiles/v_bus_positions/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf`.

3. **Add to the client (MapLibre GL JS)**:
   ```js
   const map = new maplibregl.Map({
     container: 'map',
     style: {
       version: 8,
       sources: {
         basemap: {
           type: 'raster',
           url: 'https://my-cdn.example.com/basemap/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
           tileSize: 256
         },
         buses: {
           type: 'vector',
           tiles: ['https://tiles.myhost.com/tiles/v_bus_positions/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf'],
           minzoom: 5,
           maxzoom: 18
         }
       },
       layers: [
         { id: 'basemap', type: 'raster', source: 'basemap' },
         {
           id: 'bus-points',
           type: 'circle',
           source: 'buses',
           'source-layer': 'v_bus_positions',
           paint: {
             'circle-radius': ['interpolate', ['linear'], ['zoom'], 5, 4, 12, 8],
             'circle-color': '#ff6600',
             'circle-stroke-width': 1,
             'circle-stroke-color': '#fff'
           }
         }
       ]
     },
     center: [-122.33, 47.60],
     zoom: 12
   });
   ```

4. **Cache tiles** (optional but recommended):
   - **In‑memory**: `pg_tileserv` has a `--cache-size` flag.
   - **Redis**: Use `t-rex` or `tile38` if you need a distributed cache.

5. **Refresh frequency**: Because the tiles are cached, you need to **expire** them after ~30 s.  
   - Set `Cache-Control: max-age=30` on the tile response (pg_tileserv supports `--cache-control`).  
   - Or use a **tile‑version query param** (`?v=timestamp`) that you bump on each refresh (simple client‑side trick).

### 4.2. Example: WebSocket JSON Feed

If you prefer a pure JSON push (no tiling), here’s a minimal Node/Express + `socket.io` sketch:

```js
// server.js
const express = require('express');
const http = require('http');
const { Server } = require('socket.io');
const { Pool } = require('pg');

const app = express();
const server = http.createServer(app);
const io = new Server(server, { cors: { origin: '*' } });

const pgPool = new Pool({
  connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL,
});

io.on('connection', (socket) => {
  console.log('client connected', socket.id);
});

async function broadcastBuses() {
  const { rows } = await pgPool.query(`
    SELECT bus_id,
           ST_X(geom) AS lon,
           ST_Y(geom) AS lat,
           speed,
           heading,
           last_update
    FROM bus_positions
    WHERE last_update > now() - interval '2 minutes';
  `);
  io.emit('busPositions', rows);
}

// fire every 30 s
setInterval(broadcastBuses, 30_000);
broadcastBuses(); // initial push

server.listen(3000, () => console.log('🚍 bus API on :3000'));
```

Client side (MapLibre + socket.io):

```js
const socket = io('https://api.myhost.com');

let source = map.getSource('live-buses');
if (!source) {
  map.addSource('live-buses', {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: { type: 'FeatureCollection', features: [] }
  });
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'live-bus-points',
    type: 'circle',
    source: 'live-buses',
    paint: {
      'circle-radius': 6,
      'circle-color': '#ff6600',
      'circle-stroke-width': 1,
      'circle-stroke-color': '#fff'
    }
  });
}

socket.on('busPositions', (buses) => {
  const features = buses.map(b => ({
    type: 'Feature',
    geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [b.lon, b.lat] },
    properties: {
      bus_id: b.bus_id,
      speed: b.speed,
      heading: b.heading,
      last_update: b.last_update
    }
  }));
  map.getSource('live-buses').setData({
    type: 'FeatureCollection',
    features
  });
});
```

**Pros**: No tile generation, instant updates, easy to animate heading changes.  
**Cons**: All points are sent to every client (bandwidth grows with fleet size). Use clustering or bounding‑box filters if you have > 5 k buses.

---

## 5. When PMTiles *Could* Still Be Part of the Solution

| Situation | How to use PMTiles |
|-----------|-------------------|
| **Static basemap** (streets, satellite, terrain) | Store the basemap as PMTiles on a CDN. Clients fetch it with `raster` or `vector` sources. |
| **Historical snapshots** (e.g., “bus positions at 08:00”) | Generate a PMTiles file for each snapshot and serve it as a versioned layer (`/tiles/2024-08-28-08-00/{z}/{x}/{y}.pbf`). |
| **Offline mobile app** | Bundle the PMTiles file with the app; the live bus feed can still be fetched via HTTP when online. |

In all cases, **the live bus layer stays separate**.

---

## 6. Performance & Cost Checklist

| ✅ Checklist Item | Why it matters |
|-------------------|----------------|
| **Cache static tiles aggressively** (`Cache-Control: max-age=31536000`) | Reduces CDN egress and speeds up page loads. |
| **Set a short max‑age for the live tile source** (`max-age=30`) | Guarantees the client sees fresh positions. |
| **Enable gzip/deflate or brotli for vector tiles** | Vector tiles are already compact, but compression saves bandwidth. |
| **Use a tile‑level cache (Redis, memcached) for dynamic tiles** | Avoids hitting PostGIS for every request; only recompute when data actually changed. |
| **Limit the query to recent positions** (`WHERE last_update > now() - interval '2 minutes'`) |
